WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.040
Whats up guys

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.420
Today we released a video called "Gamers React"

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:08.600
And you might not notice this, but it's actually a very special video because it's our first virtual production

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.800
Let me explain

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.220
Welcome to my chambers

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.840
We got this quarantine situation going on

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.300
We can't gather, but I can still write pieces

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.480
What can we do in that situation?

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.860
What kind of piece can we make, where people can't gather?

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.440
Well, lets do a parody of our "Visual Effects Artists React" series

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.180
Except it's video game characters talking about, I guess, what they learned from their experiences

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.440
I figured, you know what, why not learn Unreal

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.040
They put a lot of energy into making it specialized for virtual production

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.700
"Sam, why are you making a video with Unreal Engine?"

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.980
"Why not just use Cinema 4d and Octane, cause it's way higher quality?"

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.360
First off, thank you, for asking that question

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.740
There is a huge difference between making a 3D animated... helicopter

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.860
[Helicopter noises in the background]

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.840
There is a difference between a 3D animation and a virtual production

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.660
And if we wanted to make a 3D animated video, we would take a very different path

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.120
The whole point of a virtual production is to lift as many practices and techniques

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.300
From a traditional real-life film set as possible and then apply that to your virtual world

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.440
"Ya say you wanna to make a virtual production, kid?"

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.760
Well, I got the tools for you, check it out

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.180
First, we start with Unreal Engine

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.820
It's free! Wow!
Holy cow! Done!

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.300
Install that on your PC

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.820
Next up, you want an iPhone

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.360
Sorry Android users, the iPhone is way better for this kind of stuff

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.620
First off, what do we got here?

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.680
Oh man, whoa, is this a facial tracking application?

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:29.520
Yeah

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.940
This is from Epic Games, Unreal made this

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.960
This is their official facial capture application, and it works really, really well

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.120
It lets me record all my facial expressions and translate that to a digital character

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.360
Don't judge this app by my results,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.060
Because you still have to be good at making a really good facial rig for this to even function with

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.340
But this app is dope

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.240
This is top secret beta software, but I think by the time this video gets released this will be public

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.980
You're also going to want your iPhone

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.160
Because if you want to control your camera Unreal and Epic Games has already made a virtual camera app

00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:59.220
"Are you psycho?"

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:02.240
Yes, I'm psycho, I'm psycho excited for this project

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.620
Check this out, I got a virtual camera

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.460
It's tracking all my movements with my camera

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.540
Do you see that?
Look at that

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.300
This is nuts

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.340
So now I get an angle down here

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.140
Or I can get an angle up here

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.240
Do you see how crazy this is?

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.080
Cool, well that is checking two things off the list

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.560
I can take my sweet real-life acting skills and apply them

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.900
I can take my sweet real-life camera skills and apply them

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.680
"What's the last step you may ask, Sam?"

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:23.180
Well...

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.740
A $20 Thousand Dollar motion capture suit

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:27.900
[Laughter]

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.800
*A $20 Thousand Dollar motion capture suit

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.920
I love the Xsens suit, it has been working really well

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.740
These might look like actual pajamas, but trust me, you do not want to sleep in this

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:42.760
Basically this allows me to translate my physical body movements into the game engine and capture a performance

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.900
With all these tools though, I don't have to hand animate a single thing

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.140
It's not a virtual production if you are sitting there, like, tweaking all the stuff all day, it's like, no

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.440
"What does a production entail?"

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.520
A director, a camera man, some actors, and an environment

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.600
When I'm done wiggling my body around and moving the camera around I hit cut and it's there

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.380
The data is captured and I get to use it

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.640
"What about the environment?"

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:06.260
In doing a react video, in our style, well that takes place on a couch, in our studio

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.840
So thankfully recreating that was super, super easy

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.160
I needed a nice couch 3D model, but then I also needed a 3D model of our entire studio

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.160
And thankfully the guys from Leica came by our studio last year

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.360
And we got a pretty robust, full on 3D scan of our entire space

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.900
That all I had to do was optimize a little bit, for real-time, and then drop it right in

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:28.940
It's a little low poly, but it's okay, because we are never going, to like, fully see the studio in focus

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.140
It fits, you know, it feels like we are in the studio now

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.440
That's it, that's the whole environment

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.260
