00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:06.320
So part of the fun of the VFX Artist series is we all gather and we kind of riff on these clips that we're watching

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.820
It's not scripted, it's totally improvised, we're all just there, you know, explaining what we're seeing

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.200
So we wanted to make sure this video had a very similar feel to that

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:18.760
We would hop on Discord and we would basically just riff on it, for like an hour or so

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:22.580
I would take all that improvisation and then I would basically make a script

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.620
That was the foundation for this entire piece

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:28.980
Once we have our audio we would edit it, it would kind of sound like a podcast, basically

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.600
But I used that as the blueprint for everything I'm about to do

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.200
You can basically make a timeline, in Unreal, that you are going to animate all your stuff to

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.860
And you can drag in your audio tracks and see the wave-forms right here

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:39.420
It's pretty nuts

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:43.800
So now that I've got my audio files, we need our bodies to move, we need our faces to move

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.100
And then, once everyone is moving, we film it

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.040
So, I put on the motion capture suit

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:50.080
And then I basically Power Rangers it

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.520
And I do this, I'm literally just doing that while I'm listening

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.440
I'm syncing the stuff for each character, for the entire piece, we are just using Niko and Clint's voices

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.220
So, from there, we open up our facial capture application

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:02.560
Oh wow, look at me go

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.360
It looks good, but certain expressions... some break it, that's my fault here

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.860
But, now I have to do this for every take, for every character

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:11.880
Niko: "Sam, tell us a little about what you do"
[Mouthing words]

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:16.720
My lip sync is really good... Why? Well, it's because I've listened to this scene like a hundred times by now

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.320
It's really hard to just drop yourself into this scene and like understand the rhythm of all the dialog

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:22.800
Now I've recorded a face

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.420
I can basically take that file and I drop that onto my character here

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:28.600
Now, we have a character talking

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.900
Once I do the body capture and then I do the facial capture

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:36.180
What I'm left with, is basically the equivalent of the rehearsals, so to speak

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:37.740
Everyone knows their places

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.280
Everyone knows their lines

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.240
We still need to whip out the camera and film the dang thing

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.120
This is my favorite part, I've taken off the motion capture suit, I'm not lip syncing anymore

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.745
I don't have any more work to do

00:07:47.745 --> 00:07:50.900
My camera work is being transferred into the sequence

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:52.380
"How would you film this scene?"

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:55.020
Well, probably a nice punched in angle, like this

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.300
We would probably zoom out a little bit

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.640
Get maybe, like a wider shot, that has all three of us on the couch

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:03.220
Since I'm not key framing stuff, this is all captured

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:06.600
I feel like there is this really natural organic element that you get to see here

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.700
Like having this really fluid handheld camera in a virtual environment

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:10.780
I think it is pretty trippy to watch

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.880
And what we are left with, is a piece of footage

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.615
It's like we're offloading footage from a camera, at that point

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:20.460
All we need to do from there, is to take it and edit through our different camera angles

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:21.480
And get our cuts going

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:25.380
So the end product here, it's not necessarily the most 100% realistic thing

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:26.620
But it wasn't designed to be

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:28.540
We knew it wasn't going to be 100% photoreal

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.600
We knew the animation was going to be a little jankey

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:34.780
But the thing is, that all these things are kinda in place, now, for us to improve on it

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.800
And hopefully make it look a lot more realistic

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:41.900
Because there are some pretty crazy things coming out for Unreal

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:44.780
I want to take a moment to personally thank the guys at Unreal

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:48.320
Because I was able to get in touch with them, but some of them helped me step-by-step

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.800
Get a lot of this stuff built out and answering my questions

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:56.740
These guys are very passionate about getting stuff like this working, helping creators find and create workflows

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:58.460
They see the promise in this, which is

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:01.640
We have an opportunity here to create a new style of content

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:06.140
People can create things that would have been completely impossible if they had to do it on a real set

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:12.000
We're working towards this world, where your creativity and your film-making skills are the only things that are going to hold you back

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.980
It's not your budget, it's not what actors you can get, it's not the kind of environments and locations you can afford

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:21.780
It's just simply, "What's the story you're trying to tell?" and "How creative can you get with it?"

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:23.140
And that's pretty cool

